🔍 Anonymous Access to Network Services

This guide explains how to check and connect **anonymously** to common network services during CTFs, penetration testing, or red teaming.

---

## 📁 FTP (Port 21)

Try connecting anonymously with username `anonymous`.

### Manual:

ftp <ip>
```
Login:
```
Name: anonymous
Password: <any string or blank>
```

### Scripted:
```bash
ftp -n <ip>
```
Then:
```
user anonymous ""
```

---

## 📂 SMB (Ports 139, 445)

### List SMB Shares Anonymously:
```bash
smbclient -L //<ip> -N
```

### Connect to Share:
```bash
smbclient //<ip>/sharename -N
```

### Enumerate with enum4linux:
```bash
enum4linux -a <ip>
```

---

## 🖥️ Telnet (Port 23)

Try connecting (if open):

```bash
telnet <ip>
```

> Use default credentials or brute-force if login is required.

---

## 🔄 Rsync (Port 873)

### List Available Modules:
```bash
rsync rsync://<ip>/
```

### Download from a Module:
```bash
rsync -av rsync://<ip>/module_name .
```

---

## 🧑‍💻 RDP (Port 3389)

RDP usually **requires credentials**, but you can test info leakage or brute-force.

### Check RDP Info:
```bash
nmap -p 3389 --script rdp-ntlm-info <ip>
```

### Connect with Credentials:
```bash
xfreerdp /v:<ip> /u:<username> /p:<password>
```

---

## 🔧 Useful Nmap Scripts

Scan for anonymous access & service info:
```bash
nmap --script ftp-anon,ftp-bounce,smb-enum-shares,smb-enum-users -p 21,445 <ip>
```

